      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Limited arthrodesis of the proximal carpal row to the radius, synovectomy of the extensor tendons and the wrist joint, and a Darrach procedure were carried out in 25 unstable painful rheumatoid wrists that had ulnar shift and/or palmar subluxation of the carpus. The average follow-up period after the operation was 3 years. Satisfactory results were obtained with relief of pain, improved forearm rotation, and increased grip strength. The average degree of extension and flexion was reduced, respectively, 70% and 54% at follow-up compared with the preoperative range. Postoperative x-ray films showed deterioration in the lunocapitate joint in 12 wrists; however, the wrists remained stable and painless."},
